A.1–A.3 accompany [Arithmetic Expressions](chapter-01/index.html). A.4–A.5 accompany [Big-Step Semantics of Commands](chapter-02/index.html) and supply the rules used in [Inversion and Determinacy](chapter-03/index.html). In the judgment $`\sigma\vdash c\Downarrow\sigma'` the letters $`\sigma,\sigma'` stand for complete states, $`c` for a command, and the down arrow for a terminating execution. Session 4 uses the syntax of A.1 for [expression folds](chapter-04/index.html). A.6 gives the step and path judgments for [Session 5](chapter-05/index.html) and [Session 6](chapter-06/index.html). [English–Korean glossary](glossary/index.html)

*Metavariables.* $`x,y,z` range over variable names, $`n` over integers, $`e` over arithmetic expressions, $`b` over Boolean expressions and $`c,d` over commands. $`\sigma,\sigma',\sigma''` range over states and $`\sigma_1,\sigma_2` name intermediate states in command derivations. In expression judgments, $`m,n,n_1,n_2` denote integers. $`p,q,r` range over configurations.

# A.1 Arithmetic expressions · Session 1
%%%
tag := "imp-a1"
number := false
%%%

Let $`x` range over variable names (strings) and $`n` over mathematical integers. An expression is a finite tree built with four constructors:

$$`e ::= n \mid x \mid (e_1+e_2) \mid (e_1-e_2).`

This BNF-style notation describes an inductively defined syntactic category: exactly the finite trees generated by the four constructors. A minus sign at an internal node denotes binary subtraction. A negative integer at a leaf is a literal.

*Structural induction* proves a property $`P(e)` by considering each constructor. At a binary node, the claims $`P(e_1)` and $`P(e_2)` for its two immediate children are the *induction hypotheses*.

Write $`V=\mathrm{Name}` for the set of strings, $`\mathbb Z` for the set of integers and $`E` for the set of expression trees.

*Free variables.* The function $`\operatorname{FV}:E\rightarrow\mathcal{P}(V)` assigns a set of variable names to each expression. Here $`\mathcal{P}(V)` is the set of all subsets of $`V`, $`\varnothing` is the empty set, and $`\cup` denotes set union. Define

$$`\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{FV}(n)&=\varnothing & \mathrm{FV}(x)&=\{x\}\\
\mathrm{FV}(e_1+e_2)&=\mathrm{FV}(e_1)\cup\mathrm{FV}(e_2)\\
\mathrm{FV}(e_1-e_2)&=\mathrm{FV}(e_1)\cup\mathrm{FV}(e_2).
\end{aligned}`

# A.2 States and update · Session 1
%%%
tag := "imp-a2"
number := false
%%%

A state records the current integer value of every variable in IMP. We represent it by a total function $`\sigma:\mathrm{Name}\to\mathbb Z`. Write $`\Sigma` for the set of states. The update $`\sigma[x\mapsto n]` is the function whose value at $`x` is $`n` and whose value at each other name $`y` is $`\sigma(y)`. Equality of states means equality at every name.

# A.3 Expression evaluation · Session 1
%%%
tag := "imp-a3"
number := false
%%%

The evaluation function is defined by *structural recursion*. There is one equation per constructor, and recursive calls are made only on immediate children. At each binary node it obtains the two operand values in the same state.

$$`\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{eval}(n,\sigma)&=n & \mathrm{eval}(x,\sigma)&=\sigma(x)\\
\mathrm{eval}(e_1+e_2,\sigma)&=\mathrm{eval}(e_1,\sigma)+\mathrm{eval}(e_2,\sigma)\\
\mathrm{eval}(e_1-e_2,\sigma)&=\mathrm{eval}(e_1,\sigma)-\mathrm{eval}(e_2,\sigma).
\end{aligned}`

*Evaluation judgment.* Write $`\sigma\vdash e\Downarrow n` when the judgment has a finite derivation by the following rules. An *inference rule* puts its premises above the line and its conclusion below. A rule with no premises is an *axiom*. A *derivation* is a finite tree of rule applications whose root is the judgment proved and whose children derive the premise judgments. If $`D` is a derivation whose root is the judgment $`J`, we write $`D:J` and say that $`D` derives $`J`.

$$`\frac{}{\sigma\vdash n\Downarrow n}\qquad\frac{}{\sigma\vdash x\Downarrow\sigma(x)}`

$$`\frac{\sigma\vdash e_1\Downarrow n_1\quad \sigma\vdash e_2\Downarrow n_2}{\sigma\vdash e_1+e_2\Downarrow n_1+n_2}
\qquad\frac{\sigma\vdash e_1\Downarrow n_1\quad \sigma\vdash e_2\Downarrow n_2}{\sigma\vdash e_1-e_2\Downarrow n_1-n_2}`

We refer to the four rules as the literal axiom, the variable axiom, the addition rule and the subtraction rule. Relative to the evaluator, soundness says every derivable result is the computed result; completeness says the computed result has a derivation. [Theorem 1.3](chapter-01/index.html#ch01-s5) proves both directions.

For example, if $`\sigma(x)=2`, the variable axiom and the literal axiom supply the premises for $`\sigma\vdash x-3\Downarrow -1`. *Inversion* recovers the possible last rules of a derivation, together with the premises and equalities forced by their conclusions.

# A.4 Guards and commands · Session 2
%%%
tag := "imp-a4"
number := false
%%%

A Boolean expression has one of six forms:

$$`b ::= \mathsf{true}\mid\mathsf{false}\mid(e_1=e_2)\mid(e_1\le e_2)\mid\neg b\mid(b_1\land b_2).`

Its value $`B(b,\sigma)` is a Boolean. Constants give their own values. Comparisons test the integer values of both operands in $`\sigma`. Negation exchanges true and false. Conjunction returns true exactly when both operands return true. In programs, $`e_1<e_2` abbreviates $`\neg(e_2\le e_1)`, $`e_1\ne e_2` abbreviates $`\neg(e_1=e_2)`, and $`b_1\lor b_2` abbreviates $`\neg(\neg b_1\land\neg b_2)`.

$$`c ::= \mathsf{skip}\mid x:=e\mid(c_1;c_2)\mid
\mathsf{if}\ b\ \mathsf{then}\ c_1\ \mathsf{else}\ c_2\mid
\mathsf{while}\ b\ \mathsf{do}\ c.`

The Boolean expression controlling a conditional or loop is its *guard*.

# A.5 Big-step execution · Session 2
%%%
tag := "imp-a5"
number := false
%%%

In a *big-step semantics*, a judgment relates an entire computation to its result. The following six rules define $`\sigma\vdash c\Downarrow\sigma'`. The judgment holds precisely when it has a finite derivation. Skip leaves the state unchanged. Assignment evaluates the right-hand side in the initial state. *Sequencing* runs the second command from the final state of the first: the sequence rule binds its two premises through the intermediate state $`\sigma_1`.

$$`\frac{}{\sigma\vdash\mathsf{skip}\Downarrow\sigma}\qquad
\frac{}{\sigma\vdash x:=e\Downarrow\sigma[x\mapsto\mathrm{eval}(e,\sigma)]}`

$$`\frac{\sigma\vdash c\Downarrow\sigma_1\quad \sigma_1\vdash d\Downarrow\sigma'}{\sigma\vdash(c;d)\Downarrow\sigma'}`

For $`I=\mathsf{if}\ b\ \mathsf{then}\ c\ \mathsf{else}\ d`, let $`k` denote $`c` when $`B(b,\sigma)` is true and $`d` otherwise. There is one conditional rule:

$$`\frac{\sigma\vdash k\Downarrow\sigma'}{\sigma\vdash I\Downarrow\sigma'}`

Fixing the value of $`B(b,\sigma)`, as true or as false, gives a *derived rule*: each of its instances follows by one application of the defining rule. The guard $`b` itself is not replaced.

Write $`W=\mathsf{while}\ b\ \mathsf{do}\ c`. The guard tests above the lines below are *side conditions*. Evaluate the Boolean function to check each equality. Only the execution judgments above a line, the rule's execution premises, require subderivations. A false guard ends the loop. A true guard requires both an execution of the body and a further execution of the loop starting in the body’s final state.

$$`\frac{B(b,\sigma)=\mathsf{false}}{\sigma\vdash W\Downarrow\sigma}\qquad
\frac{B(b,\sigma)=\mathsf{true}\quad \sigma\vdash c\Downarrow\sigma_1\quad \sigma_1\vdash W\Downarrow\sigma'}{\sigma\vdash W\Downarrow\sigma'}`

We refer to the six rules as the skip, assignment, sequence, conditional, loop exit and loop iteration rules.

The *height of an execution derivation* is zero when its last rule has no execution premises. Otherwise it is one plus the greatest height of an immediate execution subderivation.

# A.6 Transitions and paths · Session 5
%%%
tag := "imp-a6"
number := false
%%%

A *configuration* $`\langle c,\sigma\rangle` consists of a residual command $`c` (the work still to do) and the current state $`\sigma`. Write $`p\to q` for one transition. Assignment evaluates its right-hand side in the current state and steps to skip with the updated state.

$$`\frac{}{\langle x:=e,\sigma\rangle\to\langle\mathsf{skip},\sigma[x\mapsto\mathrm{eval}(e,\sigma)]\rangle}`

A sequence with skip on the left steps to its right command. A step of its left command lifts through the sequence, carrying the resulting state with it.

$$`\frac{}{\langle \mathsf{skip};d,\sigma\rangle\to\langle d,\sigma\rangle}
\qquad\frac{\langle c,\sigma\rangle\to\langle c',\sigma'\rangle}{\langle c;d,\sigma\rangle\to\langle c';d,\sigma'\rangle}`

For $`I=\mathsf{if}\ b\ \mathsf{then}\ c\ \mathsf{else}\ d`, let $`k` be the branch selected by $`B(b,\sigma)` as in Session 2, Definition 2.1. For $`W=\mathsf{while}\ b\ \mathsf{do}\ c`, a false guard exits and a true guard exposes the body.

$$`\frac{}{\langle I,\sigma\rangle\to\langle k,\sigma\rangle}\qquad
\frac{B(b,\sigma)=\mathsf{false}}{\langle W,\sigma\rangle\to\langle\mathsf{skip},\sigma\rangle}\qquad
\frac{B(b,\sigma)=\mathsf{true}}{\langle W,\sigma\rangle\to\langle c;W,\sigma\rangle}`

Skip itself has no outgoing step. We refer to the six rules as the small-step assignment rule, the skip-discarding rule, the lifting rule, and the small-step conditional, loop exit and loop iteration rules.

A finite path $`p\to^*q` either takes zero steps with $`p=q`, or has a first step $`p\to r` and a finite tail $`r\to^*q`. Write $`p\to^n q` when there are configurations $`p_0,\ldots,p_n` with $`p_0=p`, $`p_n=q` and $`p_i\to p_{i+1}` for every $`0\le i<n`. The natural number $`n` counts edges.

An *infinite path* from $`p` is witnessed by a function $`f` from natural numbers to configurations with $`f(0)=p` and $`f(i)\to f(i+1)` for every $`i`. The coinductive presentation uses a predicate on configurations: the greatest predicate $`X`, under inclusion, satisfying $`X(p)\iff\exists q,\ p\to q\land X(q)`.

# Source and proof references
%%%
tag := "imp-sources"
number := false
%%%

These references document the formalization. They are optional for seminar preparation. [Lean source index](lean/index.html)

*Source.* The shared definitions follow the Lean port of Xavier Leroy’s _Compiler Verification_ course at revision `a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01`. The evaluation judgment in [A.3](imp-card/index.html#imp-a3) is formulated in this book on the same syntax. [IMP definitions](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Imp.lean) · [Paths](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Sequences.lean)

* A state $`\sigma` has type [`store`](lean/Language.html#store) in Lean. $`\mathrm{eval}(e,\sigma)`, $`B(b,\sigma)` and $`\sigma[x\mapsto n]` correspond to [`aeval s e`](lean/Language.html#aeval), [`beval s b`](lean/Language.html#beval) and [`update x n s`](lean/Language.html#update).
* $`\sigma\vdash c\Downarrow\sigma'` corresponds to [`cexec s c t`](lean/Language.html#cexec). The conditional rule is [`cexec_ifthenelse`](lean/Language.html#cexec_ifthenelse).
* One step is [`red`](lean/Language.html#red). The finite closure is [`star`](lean/Language.html#star). The two presentations of an infinite path are [`infseq_with_function`](lean/Language.html#infseq_with_function) and [`infseq`](lean/Language.html#infseq).
* Definition 1.5 is [`PLBook.IMP.Evaluates`](lean/Arithmetic.html#Evaluates). In [`PLBook.IMP.Arithmetic`](lean/Arithmetic.html), membership in the free-variable set is [`occurs`](lean/Arithmetic.html#occurs). Lemma 1.1 is [`coincidence`](lean/Arithmetic.html#coincidence), Corollary 1.2 is [`updateIrrelevant`](lean/Arithmetic.html#updateIrrelevant), Theorem 1.3 is [`correspondence`](lean/Arithmetic.html#correspondence), and Corollary 1.4 is [`uniqueValue`](lean/Arithmetic.html#uniqueValue), all in the namespace [`PLBook.IMP`](lean/index.html). Expression substitution and its evaluation lemma are [Definition 11.1 and Lemma 11.3](chapter-11/index.html#ch11-s1).
* Proposition 2.2 is [`PLBook.IMP.sequenceExecution`](lean/Determinacy.html#sequenceExecution) on the shared execution relation.
* The proof module [`PLBook.IMP.Determinacy`](lean/Determinacy.html) checks Lemma 3.1 with [`PLBook.IMP.assignmentResult`](lean/Determinacy.html#assignmentResult) and Theorem 3.2 with [`PLBook.IMP.deterministic`](lean/Determinacy.html#deterministic) on the shared execution relation. The two derived conditional rules are [`PLBook.IMP.ifTrue`](lean/Determinacy.html#ifTrue) and [`PLBook.IMP.ifFalse`](lean/Determinacy.html#ifFalse).
* Theorem 4.1 is [`PLBook.IMP.uniqueFold`](lean/Fold.html#uniqueFold) in [`PLBook.IMP.Fold`](lean/Fold.html). It includes existence and uniqueness for all four constructors of [`aexp`](lean/Language.html#aexp). The operations take values in an arbitrary type.
* In [`PLBook.IMP.Paths`](lean/Paths.html), Lemma 5.3 is [`progress`](lean/Paths.html#progress) on the shared transition relation. The source statement is [`red_progress`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Imp.lean#L317). [Progress for IMP commands](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Imp.lean#L315-L335)
* Lemma 5.4 is [`finiteIffLength`](lean/Paths.html#finiteIffLength). The finite sequence in $`p\to^n q` is represented by [`PathOfLength`](lean/Paths.html#PathOfLength): a single configuration at length zero, or a first edge followed by a sequence of length $`n` at length $`n+1`. Example 5.5 is [`skipLoopInfinite`](lean/Paths.html#skipLoopInfinite) on [`infseq_with_function`](lean/Language.html#infseq_with_function). Its checked witness alternates between the loop head and its unfolded body.
* Lemma 6.1 is [`liftLength`](lean/Paths.html#liftLength) and preserves the exact length. Leroy’s [`red_seq_steps`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Imp.lean#L342) lifts a finite path without specifying its length. [Sequence context in IMP](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Imp.lean#L340-L356)
* Theorem 6.2 is [`terminatingEquivalence`](lean/Paths.html#terminatingEquivalence). The source gives the two implications separately in [`cexec_to_reds`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Imp.lean#L363) and [`reds_to_cexec`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Imp.lean#L399). Lemma 6.3 is [`executionBeforeStep`](lean/Paths.html#executionBeforeStep), with the same premise and conclusion as [`red_append_cexec`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Imp.lean#L390). These book proofs use the shared IMP relations. [Forward implication](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Imp.lean#L361-L385) · [Backward step and reverse implication](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Imp.lean#L388-L405)
* In [`PLBook.IMP.Equivalence`](lean/Equivalence.html), Theorem 7.3 is [`loopUnfolding`](lean/Equivalence.html#loopUnfolding) and Proposition 7.4 is [`equivalentBeforeSuffix`](lean/Equivalence.html#equivalentBeforeSuffix).
* In [`PLBook.IMP.Denotation`](lean/Denotation.html), an outcome is an optional IMP state. The strict extension, flat order, pointwise order and finite approximations adapt the definitions in the seminar’s `Lecture/S02Order.lean` to arbitrary IMP guards and body meanings. Theorem 8.5 is [`sequencingAssoc`](lean/Denotation.html#sequencingAssoc), Corollary 8.6 is [`commandSequencingAssoc`](lean/Denotation.html#commandSequencingAssoc), Proposition 8.8 is [`sequencingMono`](lean/Denotation.html#sequencingMono), Lemma 8.10 is [`outcomeChainCases`](lean/Denotation.html#outcomeChainCases) and Proposition 8.11 is [`chainSup_isLub`](lean/Denotation.html#chainSup_isLub).
* In the same module, Lemmas 9.2 and 9.3 are [`unfoldMono`](lean/Denotation.html#unfoldMono) and [`approxIncreasing`](lean/Denotation.html#approxIncreasing). Theorem 9.5 is [`whileDenotationLeastFixed`](lean/Denotation.html#whileDenotationLeastFixed). Its loop denotation is the pointwise supremum of the finite approximations.
* Lemma 10.1 is [`intermediateStore`](lean/Denotation.html#intermediateStore) in [`PLBook.IMP.Denotation`](lean/Denotation.html). In [`PLBook.IMP.Correspondence`](lean/Correspondence.html), Theorem 10.2 is [`executionIffDenotation`](lean/Correspondence.html#executionIffDenotation) and Corollary 10.3 is [`graphAndDivergence`](lean/Correspondence.html#graphAndDivergence).
* In [`PLBook.IMP.Hoare`](lean/Hoare.html), Lemmas 11.3 and 11.6 are [`evalSubst`](lean/Hoare.html#evalSubst) and [`writtenSubst`](lean/Hoare.html#writtenSubst). The two clauses of Theorem 11.7 are [`assignmentValid`](lean/Hoare.html#assignmentValid) and [`writtenAssignmentValid`](lean/Hoare.html#writtenAssignmentValid). Proposition 11.8 is [`assignmentPrecondition`](lean/Hoare.html#assignmentPrecondition). The two rules of Proposition 11.9 are [`validSequence`](lean/Hoare.html#validSequence) and [`validConsequence`](lean/Hoare.html#validConsequence).
* Theorem 12.3 is [`hoareSound`](lean/Hoare.html#hoareSound) for the six rules of [`Hoare`](lean/Hoare.html#Hoare). Theorem 13.3 is [`totalHoareSound`](lean/Hoare.html#totalHoareSound) for [`TotalHoare`](lean/Hoare.html#TotalHoare), whose loop rule has a premise for each natural-number value of the variant. Both declarations are in [`PLBook.IMP.Hoare`](lean/Hoare.html).
* Sessions 17–18 follow the constant propagation chapter of the course. An abstract state is a [`Store`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L220). The relation $`\sigma\in\gamma(A)` is [`matches' s A`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L221). The order, the join and $`\{\}` are [`Le`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L228), [`Join`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L233) and [`Top`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L231). $`\mathrm{eval}^{\sharp}` and $`B^{\sharp}` are [`Aeval`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L293) and [`Beval`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L300), the update $`A[x\mapsto a]` is [`Update`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L355), and $`f^{\sharp}_{c}(A)` is [`Cexec A c`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L431), whose loop clause iterates at most [`num_iter = 20`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L403) times. Lemma 17.3 is [`matches_Le`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L245), [`Le_Join_l`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L266), [`Le_Join_r`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L268) and [`Le_Top`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L249). Lemma 17.5 is [`Aeval_sound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L312) and [`Beval_sound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L327). Lemma 17.7 is [`fixpoint_sound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L411). Theorem 17.8 is [`Cexec_sound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L447). [Constant propagation](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean)
* The simplifying constructors of Definition 18.1 are [`mk_PLUS`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L43), [`mk_MINUS`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L58), [`mk_EQUAL`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L117), [`mk_LESSEQUAL`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L123), [`mk_NOT`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L129) and [`mk_AND`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L136), with the auxiliary [`mk_PLUS_CONST`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L30). Definitions 18.2–18.3 are [`cp_aexp`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L532), [`cp_bexp`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L541), [`mk_IFTHENELSE`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L168), [`mk_WHILE`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L174) and [`cp_com`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L582). Lemma 18.1 is [`mk_PLUS_sound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L95), [`mk_MINUS_sound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L103), [`mk_EQUAL_sound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L144), [`mk_LESSEQUAL_sound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L149), [`mk_NOT_sound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L154) and [`mk_AND_sound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L159). Lemma 18.2 is [`cp_aexp_sound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L555) and [`cp_bexp_sound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L563). Lemma 18.3 is [`cexec_mk_IFTHENELSE`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L179), [`cexec_mk_WHILE_done`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L185) and [`cexec_mk_WHILE_loop`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L191). Theorem 18.4 is [`cp_com_correct_terminating`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Constprop.lean#L602).
* Sessions 19–20 follow the liveness and dead code chapter. A set of names is an [`IdentSet`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L13), a hash set. The development assumes one axiom, [`union_characterisation`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L25): a name is in a union exactly when it is in one of the two sets. $`\mathrm{FV}(b)` and $`\mathrm{RV}(c)` are [`fv_bexp b`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L48) and [`fv_com c`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L57). $`\mathrm{iter}(F,D,n,X)` is [`deadcode_fixpoint_rec F D n X`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L70), and $`\mathrm{iter}(F,D,20,\varnothing)` is [`deadcode_fixpoint F D`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L77). $`\mathrm{live}(c,L)` is [`live c L`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L100). Lemma 19.4 is [`fixpoint_charact'`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L80) and [`fixpoint_upper_bound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L88). Lemma 19.5 is [`live_upper_bound`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L117). Theorem 19.6 is [`live_while_charact`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L123). [Liveness and dead code](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean)
* $`\mathrm{elim}(c,L)` is [`dce c L`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L133), and $`\sigma|_X=\sigma''|_X` is [`agree X s u`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L147). Lemma 20.1 is [`beval_agree`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L171). Theorem 20.2 is [`dce_correct_terminating`](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Deadcode.lean#L208).
